Optional — no more nulls.

Several stream terminals — find-first, min, max, the one-argument reduce — hand back an optional, not a bare value. Optional is Java's answer to the billion-dollar mistake. Instead of returning null to mean "there might be no value here", and hoping the caller checks, you return a box that visibly may or may not contain a value — and the type system forces the caller to deal with the empty case.

Consider the problem it replaces. A method that returns null lies about its type. Its signature says user, but sometimes it's null, and nothing warns the caller. So you write user u equals find-user, then print u dot name — and you get a null-pointer exception, the classic, at runtime, far from where the value actually went missing. Optional makes the "might be absent" part of the type, so it can't be silently ignored. Optional of user — the type itself says "maybe a user".

Let's create and inspect one. Three factories make an optional. Optional dot of, for a present value — it throws if you pass null. Optional dot of-nullable, which is present if the reference isn't null and empty otherwise — that's your bridge from null into optional. And optional dot empty, for a deliberately empty one. To inspect, there's is-present, which gives a boolean, and get, which returns the value but throws if empty — and you should avoid get. In fact, the pairing "if is-present, then get" works, but it misses the entire point. It's the null-check you were trying to escape, just wearing a different coat. The real value of optional is the methods that keep you inside the box.

So here's the right way: transform, and supply a fallback, without ever unwrapping. Treat an optional like a stream of zero-or-one elements. The same functional operations apply, and they let you compute with a value that might not be there. So find-user, then map with user-name — that transforms if present, and you still have an optional of string. Then filter, keeping it only if the name isn't blank. Then or-else, "anonymous", which supplies a default if empty — and now you have a plain string.

Look at those pieces. Map applies a function only if a value is present, leaving empty untouched — no null check needed. Filter can empty a present optional that fails a test. And flat-map chains a call that itself returns an optional, avoiding an optional of an optional.

Then, at the edge, you leave the box by supplying what to do when empty. Or-else gives a fallback value. Or-else-get gives a fallback computed lazily, only if empty — use that when the default is expensive. Or-else-throw demands the value, throwing if it's absent. And if-present, or if-present-or-else, runs an action for the present case, and optionally the empty case.

Finally, the discipline — where optional belongs, and where it doesn't. Optional is designed for one job: a return type that signals "possibly no result", most naturally from a method or a stream terminal. Use it there, and callers get a compiler-enforced nudge to handle absence. But it is not a general nullable wrapper. Don't use optional for fields or constructor parameters — it adds a wrapper object where a plain nullable reference is clearer and cheaper. Don't put it in collections — an empty list already means "nothing", so a list of optionals is just noise. Never call get without proving presence. And never return null from a method that returns optional — an "empty or null" optional is the worst of both worlds.

Used with that discipline, optional turns a whole class of runtime null-pointer exceptions into compile-time obligations. And that is exactly why the Stream API hands you one, wherever a result might legitimately not exist.
